.TH WIILAND-CONFIG 1 "August 2026" "wiiland" "WiiLand Manual"
.SH NAME
wiiland-config \- configure and inspect the WiiLand input service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wiiland-config
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wiiland-config
is the Qt 6 control center for WiiLand. It edits Wii Remote profiles and
per-device rules, runs
.B wiilandd
readiness and diagnostic commands, captures validation traces, and controls the
installed user service.
.PP
The frontend does not implement an input backend or start a private daemon.
WiiLand input remains display-server-neutral through Linux uinput. Command,
configuration, and service failures are reported in the window.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The control center starts with the default user configuration path:
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
or, when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset,
.B $HOME/.config/wiiland/wiilandd.conf .
On startup and when
.B Load effective config
is pressed, it runs
.B wiilandd --dump-config
for that path. Because the default path is not passed as
.B --config ,
this loads the daemon's effective hierarchy: optional
.B /etc/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
first, then the optional user file, with later values overriding earlier ones.
.PP
Another key=value file may be selected in the control center. A selected path
different from the default is routed to config-sensitive daemon commands as
.B --config
that path; it is loaded as that file alone rather than as the system-plus-user
hierarchy. The selected path is also the save target. Commands that do not
consume configuration, such as device listing and axis-map, are not given this
option.
.PP
The form buttons are
.B Load effective config ,
.B Validate and save ,
and
.B Save and restart daemon
.
Loading replaces the form with the daemon's dumped effective values. Saving
first writes the rendered form to a temporary file and runs
.B wiilandd --check-config --config
against that temporary file. Only a successful check permits an atomic replace
of the selected file; a failed check leaves the existing file unchanged.
.SH SERVICE CONTROL
The daemon panel controls the installed
.B wiilandd.service
user unit with
.B systemctl --user
.
.B Refresh
runs
.B systemctl --user is-active wiilandd.service
.
.B Start ,
.B Stop ,
and
.B Restart
run the corresponding
.B systemctl --user
operation on that unit. The panel reports checking, active, inactive,
activating, deactivating, reloading, unavailable, and failed states and does
not substitute a directly spawned process when systemd or the unit is
unavailable.
.PP
The unit is installed with
.B WantedBy=default.target
and validates configuration with
.B ExecCondition=wiilandd --check-config
before starting. A successful
.B Validate and save
operation on the default user path reports that a daemon restart is required.
The
.B Save and restart daemon
button invokes the exact user-unit restart after that file is saved; the
ordinary save flow offers
.B Restart daemon
or
.B Later
.
.PP
For an explicit path, the installed user unit cannot consume the selected
file because it starts the daemon without
.BR --config .
The restart action is therefore disabled, and the control center displays the
exact
.B wiilandd --config
command required to apply the saved file.
.SH DISPLAY BACKEND
The interface uses Qt Platform Abstraction (QPA) and contains no X11 or Wayland
display-server API calls. Qt chooses a platform plugin from the session and its
own configuration; the status area reports
.B Wayland
for a
.B wayland*
platform name and
.B X11
for
.B xcb
.
.PP
To require the native Wayland plugin, run:
.PP
.RS
.B QT_QPA_PLATFORM=wayland wiiland-config
.RE
.PP
To require the Qt X11/xcb plugin, run:
.PP
.RS
.B QT_QPA_PLATFORM=xcb wiiland-config
.RE
.PP
These settings select a Qt plugin; the application does not automatically
change or retry the selection. The requested plugin must be installed and able
to connect to the session display. This QPA choice affects the control-center
window only; the
.B wiilandd
uinput path remains shared by Wayland, X.org, SDL, Steam, Wine/Proton, and
native consumers.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
Optional system-wide daemon defaults used by the daemon's default hierarchy.
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
Per-user overrides and the control center's default editable path.
.TP
.B $HOME/.config/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
Per-user fallback when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset.
.TP
.B res/70-wiiland-uinput.rules
udev permissions for hid-wiimote event nodes and
.BR /dev/uinput .
.TP
.B res/70-udev-wiiland.rules
udev permissions for writable wiimote LED sysfs attributes.
.TP
.B res/50-xorg-fix-wiiland.conf
X.org InputClass policy that suppresses matching raw Wii Remote nodes while
leaving libxwiimote and
.B WiiLand Virtual *
devices unaffected.
.TP
.B wiilandd.service
User unit installed in a systemd user-unit search path, with
.B WantedBy=default.target
and a pre-start
.B --check-config
condition.
.TP
.B wiilandd-hardware-report
Installed command for finite host, permission, daemon, doctor, device, and
manual validation diagnostics; with a device argument it continues to a live
dry-run trace.
.TP
.B /dev/uinput
Kernel uinput device used by the daemon, not by the frontend itself.
.SH PERMISSIONS
The control center does not elevate privileges. The selected configuration file
and its parent directory must be writable to save changes. The daemon must be
allowed to read hid-wiimote input event nodes and write
.BR /dev/uinput .
Install both distribution udev rules and use their normal logind, udev, or
input/uinput group policy. Permission failures remain visible as command or
service failures.
.SH DEVICE NAME COMPATIBILITY
Older releases exposed
.B WiiLand Wayland Controller
and
.B WiiLand Wayland Desktop
.
Current virtual devices are named
.B WiiLand Virtual Controller
and
.B WiiLand Virtual Desktop
in both Wayland and X.org sessions. Applications or stored controller profiles
that match device names may need remapping after upgrading.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates that the frontend exited normally. A nonzero status indicates
that the frontend could not start or terminated abnormally. Failures from a
diagnostic command, trace, configuration check, or service operation are shown
in the interface and do not by themselves terminate the frontend.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wiilandd (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR wiiland (7)
